 Console mode changes as on a Switch put in or taken out of its dock. While a TV shows the game it stays **Docked**, and the mode you pick applies when the TV is disconnected. **Docked** removes the Tap layout, as a docked Switch reads no touch screen.
 
+**Controller layout** › **Adjust Gamepad Layout** (2D and 3D) lets you move and resize the touch controls by groups: the left shoulders (ZL, L, L3), the right ones (ZR, R, R3), the left stick, the D-pad, A B X Y, and −, the gear and +. Tap a group to choose it, drag it anywhere (it follows once your finger has moved a few points, and snaps to the edges and the middle), and pinch it with two fingers on it, or use the panel's slider or − and +, to resize it. A thumb resting elsewhere on the screen does not get in the way of a drag, and a touch on the panel never moves a group under it. **All controls** resizes every control at once, down to the size that keeps every touch target 44 points across (on most iPhones held sideways at the small or medium size, − and + are no larger than that already, so it starts at 100 %). Above the chosen group's row the panel asks how a group and its mirror image move, the shoulders of both sides and the left stick with A B X Y: **Both sides together** (at first) moves them as one, so one drag lowers the whole top row, and a size goes to the mirror image as far as it has room, the chosen group growing on alone beyond that (A B X Y larger than the left stick, which meets the D-pad in 3D), the panel naming the group that stopped; **Each side on its own** moves and sizes only the group you drag. The choice is kept per layout, and it is dimmed while the D-pad or −, the gear and + is chosen, which have no mirror image. Picked while the two sides are apart, **Both sides together** moves nothing at once: the other side goes to the mirrored place the next time you move or resize either. A spot with no room turns red and the group goes back when you let go (the panel says when only the mirror image has no room there), and a size stops where the group meets another. **Reset to default** puts the layout on screen back, **Cancel** keeps the layout from before, **Done** saves it (nothing, when nothing changed). While the editor is open the game is told it lost the focus, as for the quit prompt, so a game that pauses there pauses, and the touch controls send nothing. Places are kept per layout (2D, 3D, over the picture or below it) as positions in the area each group may use, so they fit every screen; a group with no room on a smaller screen stays where it is built in. In the NX2Apple shell, **Save for** chooses all games or this game: the editor shows what Done saves there, the layout on screen and the ones you changed as you left them, the others as that choice holds them, so saving for all games from a game with its own layout replaces only those in every game's layout. For this game, **Reset to default** asks: **Built-in layout** (the layout on screen) or **Same as All games** (the game follows the layout of all games again, which Done leaves as it is). The value is `DM_TOUCH_GROUPS` (`DmPadGeometry.h`, Groups).
+
 ## TV
 
 Connect a TV or monitor by cable, or start Screen Mirroring to an Apple TV or AirPlay TV. The game fills it in 16:9 at up to 1080p and switches to **Docked**. The iPhone or iPad keeps its touch controls. With a physical controller connected, the touch controls and settings gear hide. Disconnect the controller to use touch again, or disconnect the TV to bring the picture and gear back to the device in its earlier mode. AirPlay adds some delay; a cable has almost none.
